### Description

Hey there 👋 

I was using `streamObject` with `output: "array"` in a server action to stream a bunch of llm generated questions to my nextjs client and although everything was working perfectly on my desktop, when I went to test the app on my phone, it wasn't working. 

I had tested on mobile viewports locally and in production so I was very confused as to why from an actual phone it would be any different. After some more testing I though it must be an actual phone difference, maybe something to do with the headers sent on the server action request?

I pulled up the site in production with an iphone simulator on my mac looking at logs and requests on safari dev tools and I could see the error being about how I was iterating over the stream chunks with a `for await...of`. This was working totally fine when loading the app on desktop but would break on mobile for some reason. 

My theory was the the `streamObject` response was for some reason different when the request came from mobile. So I decided to wrap the response and transform it to ensure always the same stream format. 

Before:
```ts
export async function createQuestions(userProfile: string) {
    try {
      const { elementStream } = streamObject({
        output: "array",
        model: openai("gpt-4o-mini"),
        schema: z.object({ ... }),
        system: '...',
        prompt: '...'
      });

      // Validate the stream
      if (!elementStream) {
        throw new Error('No stream returned from streamObject');
      }

      // Return the raw stream
      return elementStream;
    } catch (error) {
      console.error("Error in createQuestions:", error);
      throw error;
    }
  }
```

After:
```ts
export async function createQuestions(userProfile: string) {
  try {
    const { elementStream } = streamObject({
      output: "array",
      model: openai("gpt-4o-mini"),
      schema: z.object({ ... }),
      system: '...',
      prompt: '...'
    });

    if (!elementStream) {
      throw new Error("No stream returned from streamObject");
    }

    // Create a TransformStream to standardize the output
    const transformStream = new TransformStream({
      async transform(chunk, controller) {
        // Ensure each chunk is properly formatted
        if (typeof chunk === "object") {
          controller.enqueue(chunk);
        } else {
          try {
            // Handle cases where chunk might be text/buffer
            const parsed = JSON.parse(chunk);
            controller.enqueue(parsed);
          } catch (e) {
            console.error("Error parsing chunk:", e);
            // You might want to handle this differently based on your needs
          }
        }
      },
    });

    // Pipe through the transformer to standardize the stream
    const standardizedStream = elementStream.pipeThrough(transformStream);

    // Convert to ReadableStream if it isn't already
    if (!(standardizedStream instanceof ReadableStream)) {
      return new ReadableStream({
        async start(controller) {
          try {
            for await (const chunk of standardizedStream) {
              controller.enqueue(chunk);
            }
            controller.close();
          } catch (error) {
            controller.error(error);
          }
        },
      });
    }

    return standardizedStream;
  } catch (error) {
    console.error("Error in createQuestions:", error);
    throw error;
  }
}
```

With these changes to ensure that the action would always respond with the same format everything got fixed for both laptops and phones now. I would however have expected the `streamObject` to already respond always with the same format as the type and docs suggest.

I have managed to sort this for myself with this but thought to bring it up here so if anyone has the same issue they know how to deal with it and also so that you guys are aware this is happening in case you want to change the `streamObject` on array output mode.

Thanks!

